//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 67 - QUEUE ITEM 1, FILTER-STACK TERM 1 ON ATTACK 66. BREAKOUT-MARGIN FLOOR.
//
// THE STORED SCHEDULED PROMPT asks (again) for "Attack 37's filter stack." THE DOCS OVERRIDE IT: Attack 41
// closed Attack 37 on cost in this lab's history, Attack 43 closed the whole sweep-reversal family, and
// the live queue item 1 as of Attack 66 is "build ATTACK 66's filter stack, one term at a time, re-split
// on every addition" -- the exact instruction the stored prompt gives, aimed at the wrong, decades-stale
// mechanism number. Attack 66 (OBV/price bullish divergence, confirmed on breakout) is the second
// candidate in this lab's history with BOTH halves above 1.0 on a provable sample (137/142, PF
// 1.36461764/1.00868976) -- the same standard that earned Attack 37 its (never-built) filter-stack queue
// item, now actually being executed on the mechanism that currently holds it. This is term 1 of that
// stack, byte-identical to Attack 66 otherwise.
//
// ==================== THE TERM ====================
// BREAKOUT-MARGIN FLOOR. Attack 66's `breakoutTrigger` fires on any `ta.crossover(close, lastPivotHigh)`,
// including a close that clears the pivot high by a single tick. A bare crossover cannot distinguish a
// decisive break from a whipsaw poke that closes back below the level next bar -- and H2's razor-thin
// margin (PF 1.00868976, win rate 56.3% against a breakeven need of ~56.1%) is exactly the shape a
// population diluted by low-conviction breakouts would produce. The term requires the breakout bar to
// clear the pivot high by at least `breakoutMinPct` of price, not merely cross it.
//
// WHY THIS DOES NOT MOVE THE TARGET (HARD LESSON 46/47 distinction). The target is `close + swingAmp`,
// where `swingAmp` is fixed geometry from the pivot spread, independent of how far above the pivot high
// the entry closes. Raising the margin floor changes WHICH setups are admitted (declining the marginal
// ones), exactly the Attack 46 pattern that raised a floor on the setup's own already-offered geometry
// and saw the achieved ratio rise for free -- not the Attack 41/42/WF-E73 pattern that raised the target
// itself and paid for it in win rate. `avgBarsWinning` is checked post-run to confirm this did not buy
// reward with hold time either.
//
// WHY THIS IS ARGUABLE FROM THE MECHANISM ALONE, NOT FROM A DATE WINDOW (HARD LESSON 49). The claim --
// "a barely-clearing breakout is closer to noise than to a confirmed break" -- does not reference when
// either half's losses cluster. It was written before either half's per-trade breakout margins were
// inspected.
//
// ==================== SPLIT-FEASIBILITY CHECK BEFORE SPENDING CREDITS (HARD LESSON 45) ====================
// No free per-trade breakout-margin figure exists in `get_trades` (it returns entry/exit price and P&L,
// not the pivot-high level the entry crossed), so the cut fraction cannot be estimated for $0 the way
// HARD LESSON 45 prefers. `breakoutMinPct` is set to 0.10% -- an order of magnitude below `minRpct`
// (0.80%, the stop-distance floor) precisely so a single "barely crossed" tick is excluded without
// demanding the kind of large clearance that HARD LESSON 45/49 showed collapses a ~140-trade population
// under the 30-trade split floor. If the actual cut still lands near or above 50%, clause 4 of RATCHET v2
// applies before any KEEP.
//
// ==================== WHAT ATTACK 66 CLAIMS, IN ONE SENTENCE (unchanged, this cycle adds one term) ====================
// When price prints a confirmed pivot low BELOW its prior confirmed pivot low, but on-balance volume
// (cumulative volume flow, signed by each bar's own close-vs-prior-close direction) prints a HIGHER low
// at those same two pivots -- volume already net-accumulating while price makes a fresh low, a bullish
// divergence between price and participation -- a subsequent close back above the most recent confirmed
// pivot high confirms the divergence has resolved, and price tends to continue the reversal toward a
// measured-move projection of the swing just completed.
//
// ==================== WHY THIS IS A NEW INPUT CLASS FOR THIS LAB ====================
// Every prior mechanism used one of: rolling extremes with no structure filter (33, 48), a calendar
// anchor (34/35/54/55), a volatility state (36, 65), a failed break of a swing low (37-43), a tap-and-
// reclaim of a swing level (44-47, champion), a single bar's own range/volume (49), a session range (50),
// an RSI/PRICE divergence at a confirmed pivot (51/52), a summed order-flow-proxy ABSORPTION signal
// during a decline (53, volume used as a same-window pressure sum, not a swing-to-swing comparison), a
// consecutive-close streak (56), an EMA-slope crossover (57/58), and confirmed price-only swing structure
// (59, 63). NONE has compared a CUMULATIVE VOLUME SERIES across two confirmed pivots against the price
// series at those same two pivots. Attack 53 used volume as a same-bar/same-window pressure sum, not as
// a second independent series compared point-to-point the way Attack 51/52 compared RSI to price -- this
// is that comparison, but with volume flow (OBV) standing in for the oscillator. It is an honest
// recombination of two previously-tried ingredients (volume: 53; pivot-to-pivot divergence structure:
// 51/52/59), not a wholly unprecedented data source -- flagged here rather than overclaimed, per this
// lab's own standard of naming what a build shares with what already failed.
//
// ==================== MECHANICS, NO ARRAYS, NO USER-DEFINED FUNCTIONS ====================
// ta.pivotlow(low, 5, 5) and ta.pivothigh(high, 5, 5), confirmed 5 bars after the actual extreme, non-
// repainting. `var float` scalars (not arrays) hold the most recent and prior confirmed pivot low PRICE
// and the OBV value AT that pivot bar (ta.obv[pivotRight] read on the confirmation bar, a fixed historical
// offset, not repainting). A `var bool bullDiv` is re-evaluated (not merely set-and-forget) each time a
// new pivot low confirms: true only when price's new pivot low is BELOW the prior one AND OBV's value at
// that new pivot is ABOVE its value at the prior one (HARD LESSON 8: the divergence is armed and held in
// state, re-derived only on the pivot-confirm bar). The TRIGGER is a later, separate bar:
// `ta.crossover(close, lastPivotHigh)`, while bullDiv is latched true. Stop: the divergence low itself
// (LESSON 5, actual structure). Target: a MEASURED-MOVE PROJECTION -- breakout price plus the amplitude
// of the swing just completed (pivot high minus the divergence low) -- a level derived from the swing's
// own geometry (HARD LESSON 41), independent of R (E14: minRpct constrains the stop distance; swingAmp
// constrains the target, drawn from a different quantity).
//
// AUDIT, one line per leg (Attack 66's audit, plus the new term):
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3) - enforced by EXCLUSION via rBig, never clamped. UNCHANGED by this term.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) - slPx = lastPivLow, the actual confirmed divergence low. UNCHANGED.
//   Each leg separately (LESSON 6) - LONG ONLY. A bearish mirror is untested; the short leg is a reported
//        standing asymmetry (Attack 64). UNCHANGED.
//   BINDING (E17) - bullDiv AND breakoutTrigger AND rBig AND swingOk AND breakoutMarginOk all necessarily
//        bind; the new term is a strict narrowing of breakoutTrigger's own bar, so it can only remove
//        trades, never add any.
//   REDUNDANCY (E14) - breakoutMarginOk reads the DISTANCE by which close clears lastPivHigh; rBig reads
//        the distance from close to lastPivLow; swingAmp reads pivotHigh-minus-pivotLow. Three different
//        quantities, no overlap -- this is not the redundant-pair failure of HARD LESSON 18.
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8) - unaffected: breakoutMarginOk is evaluated on the SAME bar as
//        breakoutTrigger (both read this bar's close against lastPivHigh), so it does not introduce a new
//        same-bar setup/trigger conjunction of the kind LESSON 8 forbids -- it tightens an existing
//        single-bar trigger's own threshold, exactly like minRpct already does for rBig.
//   OCCUPANCY (LESSONS 24/28/29) - pyramiding=1, flat check gates every entry. UNCHANGED.
//   CASCADE (HARD LESSON 35/42/43) - single TP+SL, no partials, LONG at 100% equity; expect cascadeRatio 1
//        on both halves, as Attack 66 confirmed. UNCHANGED.
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY. No trailing, no averaging down, no martingale. UNCHANGED.
//
// OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17), PER THE QUEUE'S OWN STANDARD:
//   * PF IMPROVES MATERIALLY ON BOTH HALVES, drawdown does not worsen beyond RATCHET v2's allowance, count
//     stays >= 30 on each half, and any >50% cut passes a split test -> KEEP, replaces Attack 66 as base.
//   * PF IMPROVES ON ONE HALF AND WORSENS ON THE OTHER -> REJECTED per the queue's own instruction
//     ("anything that improves one half and hurts the other is rejected, not averaged"), regardless of
//     which half improves.
//   * COUNT FALLS BELOW ~30 ON EITHER HALF, OR FALLS >50% WITHOUT A PASSABLE SPLIT -> the data, not the
//     term, is the binding constraint (HARD LESSON 45/49); report as unpromotable and stop stacking on
//     this term rather than tightening or loosening the threshold to chase a pass.
//   * NEITHER HALF MOVES MATERIALLY -> the term is inert; Attack 66 stands as the base, unfalsified but
//     also unimproved.
//   REPORT GROSS, COMMISSION, avgBarsWinning/Losing, avg loser and win rate alongside PF and drawdown on
//     both halves, per this board's standing three-drawdown-category taxonomy.

strategy("BTC Attack67 OBV divergence breakout, breakout-margin floor", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

pivotLeft      = input.int(5, "Pivot confirmation bars, left")
pivotRight     = input.int(5, "Pivot confirmation bars, right")
minRpct        = input.float(0.80, "Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
maxBars        = input.int(192, "Max bars in trade")
breakoutMinPct = input.float(0.10, "Min breakout margin above pivot high, % of price (Attack 67 term)")

pl  = ta.pivotlow(low, pivotLeft, pivotRight)
ph  = ta.pivothigh(high, pivotLeft, pivotRight)
obv = ta.obv

var float lastPivLow    = na
var float prevPivLow    = na
var float lastPivLowOBV = na
var float prevPivLowOBV = na
var float lastPivHigh   = na
var bool  bullDiv       = false

if not na(pl)
    prevPivLow    := lastPivLow
    prevPivLowOBV := lastPivLowOBV
    lastPivLow    := pl
    lastPivLowOBV := obv[pivotRight]
    if not na(prevPivLow)
        bullDiv := lastPivLow < prevPivLow and lastPivLowOBV > prevPivLowOBV

if not na(ph)
    lastPivHigh := ph

rLong = not na(lastPivLow) ? close - lastPivLow : na
minR  = close * minRpct / 100
rBig  = not na(rLong) and rLong >= minR

breakoutTrigger  = not na(lastPivHigh) and ta.crossover(close, lastPivHigh)
breakoutMargin   = not na(lastPivHigh) ? close - lastPivHigh : na
minBreakoutMargin = close * breakoutMinPct / 100
breakoutMarginOk = not na(breakoutMargin) and breakoutMargin >= minBreakoutMargin

swingAmp = not na(lastPivHigh) and not na(lastPivLow) ? lastPivHigh - lastPivLow : na
swingOk  = not na(swingAmp) and swingAmp > 0

goLong = bullDiv and breakoutTrigger and breakoutMarginOk and rBig and swingOk

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

flat = strategy.position_size == 0

if flat and goLong
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    slPx   := lastPivLow
    tpPx   := close + swingAmp
    barsIn := 0

if strategy.position_size > 0
    strategy.exit("LX", from_entry="L", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goLong ? 1 : 0, title="LongSignal")
plot(lastPivLow, title="LastPivotLow")
plot(lastPivHigh, title="LastPivotHigh")
